 [{:id :s7a-hard-plan/exact-owned-allocator-contract-unselected
   :priority :P2 :context :己 :confidence 0.99
   :title "Gate 2 delegates allocator design instead of specifying the exact qualification and tool scope."
   :anchors [{:path :plan :lines [96 112]}
             {:path :plan :lines [206 214]}
             {:prefix :coordinator :path "docs/notes/oikos-execution-plan.md" :lines [625 626]}]
   :problem "The only concrete candidate is 'possibly an agent-owned transient USER scope'; authority says the parent must choose an allocator. No selected allocation entry point, exact newly owned unit/subtree identity, controller/delegation handshake, placement sequence, kill targets, or failure cleanup contract is supplied. A generic request to qualify an allocator is not yet the exact owner/tool plan required before functional changes. Existing writable i3 directories correctly grant nothing."
   :impact "The implementer cannot tell which narrowly granted OS operations are in scope, whether systemd's launcher is counted, or how to avoid touching existing managed units. This is a missing planning contract, NOT a claim that an unimplemented allocator must already pass."
   :minimal-fix "Choose ONE user-manager allocation mechanism and document its exact invocation/API properties and per-run unit naming, actual delegated-dir-FD handoff, empty controller parent/three leaves, membership and limit readback, launcher accounting, and cleanup on every failed placement/admission. Specify a grant limited to creation/configuration/kill/removal of that newly allocated agent-owned subtree and its own processes; expressly exclude i3, OCR and ancestor reconfiguration. List the owned no-socket qualification probes (startup/readback/leaf OOM/pids/cleanup) and expected failure outputs. Parent reviews that plan/tool scope before performing it; unknown/unavailable capability remains HOLD, not permission to try another allocator."
   :verification "A reviewer can enumerate every proposed unit/cgroup write and process target from the plan, without inventing paths or selecting a different mechanism. No allocator qualification is claimed by this review."}

  {:id :s7a-hard-plan/bootstrap-deadline-has-no-independent-dispatch-owner
   :priority :P2 :context :己 :confidence 0.96
   :title "Recording t0 before exec does not cover a stalled first supervisor/bootstrap startup."
   :anchors [{:path :plan :lines [108 119]}
             {:path :plan :lines [122 124]}
             {:path :plan :lines [176 182]}]
   :problem "The specification counts bootstrap/interpreter startup and requires t0 before workload exec, but names only the Python supervisor as independent timer owner. It does not identify who is already armed before the first counted supervisor/bootstrap exec, or how the immutable deadline crosses that startup. If that first exec/import/file read stalls before the supervisor loop, a stored deadline cannot itself dispatch SIGKILL. Starting t0 after supervisor initialization instead silently excludes counted bootstrap time."
   :impact "There is a concrete unmonitored startup interval even on an otherwise normally scheduled machine. This is different from the accepted unavoidable Linux dispatch/D-state limitations."
   :minimal-fix "As part of the chosen allocator, specify the already-active owner of the initial absolute deadline, the arming/readback and t0 handoff before the first counted exec, and the overlap/transfer to the nonblocking supervisor without resetting the timer. An existing manager deadline may be used only with its exact supported properties, signal/stop behavior and qualification specified; this review grants no such operation. Any extra workload watchdog process must fit the same aggregate budget and declared role/pids accounting. Add a shortened synthetic stall-before-supervisor-ready witness proving a kill request is dispatched without bootstrap cooperation."
   :verification "Startup, preflight, each case and settlement share one deadline; a blocked initial bootstrap cannot leave the client waiting indefinitely. Do not claim exact real-time death or cancellation of server inference."}

  {:id :s7a-hard-plan/second-hop-assessment-needs-real-dependency-disposition
   :priority :P2 :context :己 :confidence 0.98
   :title "The deferred assessment has a concrete installed import-time telemetry path to resolve."
